/*! \mainpage

\section summary Summary

This manual documents the interface for NDI version 2, a C, C++,
Fortran 77, and Fortran 90 callable library which provides consistent,
tested access to the best and latest nuclear data evaluations for
deterministic transport, thermonuclear burn, radiochemistry, and
production/depletion.  The following sections provide some historical
background to NDI's development, the design philosophy for the code,
the specification for the interface, and the detailed signature
specifications for all NDI version 2 interface calls grouped by
logical modules.

\section background Background

Prior to the development of the Nuclear Data Interface (NDI), users
were responsible for obtaining and accessing their own nuclear data.
This resulted in a proliferation of data files in incompatible
formats, and generally made the update of data a major, cross-project
undertaking.  Worse still, some codes embedded nuclear data in the
executable, confounding data updates with code modifications.  As a
result several FTEs were devoted, in parallel efforts, to the
maintenance and update of nuclear data.

NDI was developed in 1998 to provide a single source interface to the
best, most modern, thoroughly tested nuclear data evaluations.  It was
written in portable C, provided an interface to Fortran 77, Fortran
90, C, and C++, was ported to a number of production platforms, and
eventually supplied nuclear data to a variety of user codes.  NDI
provided more than a simple database access to nuclear data.  It
augmented access to tabulated data with a variety of user selectable
transformations and calculations.

NDI was rewritten in 2004 and released as major version 2, in response
to its growing acceptance in the user community and a desire to let
users combine nuclear data in more powerful ways.  Beginning with NDI
2.0.0, handles encapsulate NDI state, and users could create a handle
to an array of handles that could, for example, provide the data to
simulate coupled neutron-gamma multigroup transport.  Encapsulating
state also eased the use of NDI by multiple modules in one code; each
module could have its own uniquely configured handle usable without
interference from another module's calls to NDI.  Handles also
permitted the cleanup of some NDI internal logic, visible to the user
primarily in the promotion of the gendir index meta-data to a first
class object.

This document provides the design philosophy and interface
specification for NDI version 2.
 
\section design Design

NDI solves the commanded information problem for nuclear data:
provide, at the users bidding, a computer representation of nuclear
data consistent with the best empirical data and models.  Figure 1.1
shows the commanded information problem frame, where each domain
relevant to the problem is represented by a solid rectangle and the
interfaces between domains are represented by solid lines.
The domains are labeled by the type relevant to NDI in square
brackets: the user code is a Biddable domain, NDI is a Causal domain,
and user and nuclear data are both leXical domains.
 
The requirements connecting the domains are represented by dashed
lines, and the basic requirement for NDI is shown in the dashed oval:
in response to a logical request Event (E1), which may require several
physical User Code request Events (UC!E1) to explicate, make the
user's logical lexical representation of the data Y2, which may
require several NDI returns of physical lexical data (NDI!Y2),
correspond to the logical lexical representation of nuclear data Y1,
which may require several NDI reads of nuclear physical lexical data
(NDI!Y2).  NDI may satisfy its requirement by reading tabulated data
NDI!Y1 that corresponds directly to the requested user data NDI!Y2, by
calculating user requested data NDI!Y2 from other read tabulated data
with models, by transforming read or calculated data into NDI!Y2, or
by some combination of these steps.

\image latex problem.jpg "The Commanded Information Frame" width=\textwidth
\image html problem.jpg "Figure 1.1: The Commanded Information Frame"

NDI's robust response requirement adds considerable complexity to NDI's
implementation.  The following steps, labeled in Figure 1.1, are executed for
every NDI call:

-# When the user issues request E1 that NDI is required to respond to,
-# if UC!E1 is not sensible according to specification then NDI will
   return an error code,
-# or if the requested data Y1 is not available either explicitly or via
   calculation or transformation according to specification NDI will 
   return a error code or else read NDI!Y1 with a success code or
   calculate NDI!Y1 with a warning code,
-# which because the nuclear data has the real world properties,
-# that satisfy the nuclear data requirements,
-# NDI can transform into NDI!Y2 according to its state
-# and the specification of the user's data format,
-# resulting in Y2 ~ Y1 as required.

NDI organizes its data into the following types:

- multigroup_neutron: neutron deterministic transport and production
  depletion data
- multigroup_photon: photon deterministic transport data
- multigroup_multi: coupled neutron-photon deterministic transport data 
- tn: thermonuclear burn data
- tnreactions: thermonuclear burn reactions
- dosimetry_neutrons: dosimetry and radiochemistry data

Each type of data is divided into logical libraries, which collect the
data into complete sets of the best evaluations as of the library's
release date.  The data within the libraries are organized into
tables, indexed by isotope, reaction, or reaction chain identifier
depending on the data type.

The user's access of nuclear data through NDI starts with the opening
of a gendir handle, which connects the user to an Index Sequential
Access File of all of the data tables available through NDI.  The
gendir handle's state, which affects the subsequent processing of data
requests through the handle, can be set or checked, and the user can
get lists of the data tables available together with some basic
information about each table.

To access a nuclear data table the user makes a data handle of the
desired type from the gendir handle.  The data handle inherits its
state from the default settings of the gendir handle and the user can
set and check each handle's state individually.  Data handles are
typically set to retrieve data from a particular logical library and
from an isotope, reaction, or reaction chain identifier within that
library.  Once the data handle's state is set to a particular table
identifier, the user can get the data available for that table.

The user releases all memory required by NDI and closes all files
opened by NDI by closing the gendir handle.

The typical use of NDI is thus:
-# Open a gendir handle
   - optionally set or check the gendir handle state
   - optionally get data from the gendir handle
-# Make a data handle
   - optionally set or check the data handle state
   - optionally get data from the data handle
-# Close the gendir handle

\section specification Specification

Each NDI version 2 function name begins with the prefix \c NDI2_ in
the C and C++ interface, and \c ndi2_ in the Fortran77 and Fortran 90
interface, and is followed by a description of its function (open,
close, set, check, get) and its data value return type.  For example,
NDI's interface language requirement limits the user's data format to
that of the least common denominator: integers, reals, strings, and
simple arrays of these basic types, so the user's data return type is
incorporated in NDI's workhorse \c get family of functions, thus:

- \c NDI2_get_int_val* 
- \c NDI2_get_int_vec*
- \c NDI2_get_float32_val*
- \c NDI2_get_float32_vec*
- \c NDI2_get_float64_val*
- \c NDI2_get_float64_vec*
- \c NDI2_get_string_val*
- \c NDI2_get_string_vec*

The first parameter of every NDI version 2 function call is the gendir
or data handle, which is created and assigned by the \c
NDI2_open_gendir and \c NDI2_make_handle routines respectively and
destroyed by the \c NDI2_close_gendir routine.  In the set routines
the handle specifies which state the call will affect; in the check
routines the handle specifies which state the call will report; in get
routines the handle specifies which state the call will use in
reading, transforming or calculating the data.

NDI treats all state and tabulated data as named values: the second
(and possibly third) argument to every set, check, and get call is the
name (and possible index or sub-name) of the data requested.  Data
names are defined integer constants specified symbolically in the
include file \c ndi_keywords.i for Fortran 77 and Fortran 90 codes,
and in the include file \c ndi_keywords.h for C and C++ codes.  The
data name reference may be further disambiguated by an integer index
third parameter, in the case of the \c NDI2_get*_n family of calls, or
by a string sub-name third parameter, in the case of the \c
NDI2_get_*_x family of calls.

The remaining parameters specify the set, check, or get data value,
and the request return status in Fortran 77 and Fortran 90.  Vector
valued data is followed by its user allocated maximum size, as is
string valued data for C and C++ calls.  The actual size of returned
vector or string data is available through the \c NDI2_get_size_*
family of functions.

Since NDI is required to do the user's bidding, it is required to deal
with user calls gracefully, regardless of their nature.  To meet this
requirement, every NDI C call returns an integer status variable, and
every NDI Fortran call modifies its integer status variable \c ndi_err
with the following values:

-  +7: Bounds test failed: array terminated. 
-  +6: Unexpected data (too many data items).
-  +5: Unexpected zero quantity.
-  +4: Trying to close NULL stream. 
-  +3: Unable to close NDI directory file.
-  +2: NDI not initialized before NDI_close. No action taken.
-  +1: NDI already initialized. No action taken.
-  +0: Call successfully executed
-  -1: Error opening file.
-  -2: NDI not initialized before NDI library call.
-  -3: Error closing file.
-  -4: Invalid input key to NDI user function.
-  -5: Invalid key argument to NDI user function.
-  -6: Index parameter sent to NDI user function out of range.
-  -7: Isotope not set.
-  -8: Isotope not found in gendir.
-  -9: Data not available in current library for current zaid.
- -10: 32-bit function is not implemented on current platform.
- -11: Error reading file.
- -12: Unexpected library data.
- -13: Invalid input parameter.
- -14: No memory available.
- -15: Invalid data in library/directory file.
- -16: Library type not set.
- -17: Isotope not found in library.
- -18: Library error: incorrect data order.
- -19: Error setting file pointer.
- -20: Data type not set (i.e. NDI_LIB_TYPE).
- -21: Collapsing attempted but no energy structure chosen by user.
- -22: Bad zaid input.
- -23: Incompatible collapse group structure.
- -24: Bad reaction identifier (MT or C number).

In general, a value of zero indicates success, a positive error value
indicates a warning, and a negative value indicates an error.  In the
case of a warning, NDI attempts to return usable data so the user can
decide to ignore the warning and use the data anyway.  In the case of
an error, NDI returns zero numerical data or the empty string. 

The individual interface descriptions, arranged by logical modules,
are given in the Module Documentation of this document.  Under each
section the C declaration of each function is given, followed by a
brief description of its purpose.  In the Detailed Description
subsections, each function's C declaration is given, followed by a
description of its purpose an example call from Fortran 77, the
definition of its parameters, and its valid data names.

